title font5x7 -- the four diagonal arrows, GREEN; the eight-direction arrow set stands whole
prompt Run the autonomous loop: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, one council-rota doc deep-read per lap, Rye-first, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates.
think The accent-composition family closed last lap on a demonstrated boundary. The README named the next Season G rungs as the remaining wider symbols over the same 5x7 body. Crux-first among them: the arrow corpus (font5x7_arrow.rye) draws only the six basic directions -- the four cardinals and the two axis double-heads, U+2190..U+2195 -- so the diagonal corners U+2196..U+2199 are the smallest tractable move that CLOSES a set rather than opening a new open-ended one.
think A closed set is higher-Lindy than a longer catalog: the eight-direction arrow family is a thing a terminal surface can rely on being whole. This lap's rota read of SLC (item 16) sharpened the instinct -- simple (one corpus, one keystone), lovable (a surface can point off-axis to resize a corner or drag diagonally), complete (the loop the six basic arrows opened now closes). Completeness is the point, not another me-too corpus.
think Design: each arrow is a diagonal shaft stepping corner to corner (the boxdiag pen) with a two-prong head -- one horizontal stub, one vertical stub -- opening at the pointed corner so the eye reads the aim. All four land at ink 11 of 35, inside the sibling arrow band (6..16). The structural crux, parallel to boxdiag's union proof: the four are a left-right reflected family -- north-west mirrored column c to 4-c is exactly north-east, south-east is exactly south-west -- proven BOTH ways so it is a true reflection, catching a copy slip that mirrored the wrong pair.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red: 4 arrows U+2196..U+2199 round-trip byte-identical through gfa1; all present and carrying the shared advance while an ASCII letter and a cardinal arrow miss; all distinct (four corners); mirror-paired both ways; legible (ink 11..11 of 35); encoded 206 bytes over raw 140 (the diagonals are ink-scattered, so no compression, reported as fact never asserted).
obs tame_style_check GREEN (bans clean), width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII (numeric codepoint literals -- the ascii-first exception for a font codec's glyph tables). image/README.md accreted the font5x7_arrowdiag entry naming the eight-direction completion and the mirror proof (docs-sync, same commit).
loom witness=font5x7_arrowdiag_witness arrows=4 ink=11 cell_area=35 encoded_bytes=206 raw_bytes=140
think Council rota this lap (d27): the prior lap read item 4 (reds-first), so cadence continues to item 5 -- foundations/20260816-214652_standfast-the-stopped-line.md, and I also read item 16 (SLC) as it bore directly on the completeness decision. Standfast: stop the line on a real red, find the root not the symptom, resume only on a witness on metal. No red this lap; the mirror-both-ways guard is the Standfast spirit applied forward -- a class of copy slip made impossible rather than merely avoided.
file image/font5x7_arrowdiag.rye the four diagonal corner arrows grown in the open; the eight-direction set completed, mirror-paired
